WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.749
This is not a normal video.

00:00:01.749 --> 00:00:07.630
I have about 20 minutes until the boat carrying
me back to my ship leaves,

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:11.080
but I am currently on a hill in Ilulisset
in Greenland,

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.049
along with John, who works for Tele Post Greenland,

00:00:15.049 --> 00:00:22.789
and Jakob, who is my translator here, and
who is also holding my microphone inside his coat!

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:28.840
And the reason we're here is because this
is the microwave relay site for this entire town

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.769
and, by extension, everywhere north.

00:00:31.769 --> 00:00:34.790
And John here has kindly agreed to answer
a few questions.

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:38.940
This, er, this is going to be a slightly rough
video!

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.579
The largest package you can get is about four
megabits.

00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:44.079
And how long does it take you to upload something?

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:48.039
Ah, well, the upload speed is .7 megabits
per second.

00:00:48.039 --> 00:00:51.960
So it takes a horrendous amount of time to
upload anything.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.690
There's a line of sight all the way from the
south,

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.780
from mountaintop to mountaintop, all the way
up here, and way up north.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:00.680
So we should actually be able to see the other
relay tower.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.160
Where is the next relay station?

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.859
[Speaks Greenlandic]
Where is the next one?

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:04.860
[Speaks Greenlandic]
Near Qasigiannguit.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.540
Ah! All right.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:11.870
So the next relay station is about 40km south
of here, in Qasigiannguit.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:13.200
It's an unbroken link.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.980
But, of course, there can be some obstructions.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.360
[Speaks Greenlandic]

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.500
There's actually a fun little story about
the next village up,

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:28.060
it's only 8km away, but there was a huge iceberg
that

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:32.640
shadowed it from here, so they actually had
no internet this summer.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.650
But also if there's a relay station which
loses power, down south,

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.300
we might actually also lose it up here.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.310
So there was a whole day where there was no
internet up here

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:44.580
due to a relay station that had some bad batteries,
I think it was.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.780
If there's a southern station that goes out,
all the way up north, there's no signal.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.640
[Speaks Greenlandic]

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:58.400
They can't pull the sea cable away through
here, due to these massive icebergs.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.810
They scrape along the bottom of the ocean,
so for next year

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.320
they're going to upgrade all the system.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.820
They're going to pull the sea cable

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:11.680
all the way from Nuuk, all the way to Aasiaat,
which is about 120km-ish that way.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.980
And they're going to upgrade these cell towers
to carry high-speed connections,

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:20.020
all the way up to Uummannaq as well.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:26.840
[Speaks Greenlandic]

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:31.910
During the winter, these domes get covered
up fully with ice,

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:34.650
so they need to go and, er...

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.410
John, thank you so much!

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:40.190
Jakob, holding my microphone, thank you so
much!

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:41.320
You're welcome.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.100
That was not exactly the usual sort of video...

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.490
Let me turn this camera right.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:46.490
There we go!

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:49.040
That was really not the sort of usual video
that I do, but

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.090
thank you very much, Jakob here just kind
of emailed me

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.510
when I said I was coming here, and said "do
you want to film a thing?"

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.830
-- Thank you!
-- You're welcome.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:00.670
Let me move that camera... there we go.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:02.740
Thank you so much!

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:04.600
That's brilliant!

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.960
I'm here with Chris Hadfield's Generator Arctic,
and so's he!

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.670
My name is Tim Kellner, I have a YouTube channel
"Tim to the Wild",

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.430
and I make short, little cinematic travel
films.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:17.990
Go check out everything by him and all the
other folks out on this project!

